1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,770
Hey!

2
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,824
I'm Harsh Kothari, and today we're
cracking open the product rule — and
seeing why it actually works.

3
00:00:06,854 --> 00:00:07,724
Quick question.

4
00:00:07,754 --> 00:00:13,874
If you multiply two functions, is the
derivative just the derivative of each
one, multiplied together?

5
00:00:14,294 --> 00:00:17,634
Let's test it on the simplest product
there is: x times x.

6
00:00:17,734 --> 00:00:21,058
The guess says one times one — so the
slope is always one.

7
00:00:21,088 --> 00:00:23,574
But we know x squared has derivative two
x.

8
00:00:23,694 --> 00:00:26,851
At x equals three, the real slope is six,
not one.

9
00:00:26,882 --> 00:00:28,794
The guess is completely wrong.

10
00:00:29,054 --> 00:00:31,137
So what is the derivative of a product?

11
00:00:31,167 --> 00:00:33,334
The answer is hiding inside a rectangle.

12
00:00:33,729 --> 00:00:35,260
Here's the real rule.

13
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:42,129
If h of x is f of x times g of x, then h
prime equals f prime times g, plus f times
g prime.

14
00:00:42,449 --> 00:00:45,964
Prime just means derivative — the
instantaneous rate of change.

15
00:00:45,994 --> 00:00:51,509
In words: derivative of the first times
the second, plus the first times
derivative of the second.

16
00:00:51,849 --> 00:00:57,569
By the end, you'll be able to explain why
it's true using a growing rectangle — and
use it on any product.

17
00:00:57,967 --> 00:00:59,384
Picture a rectangle.

18
00:00:59,414 --> 00:01:04,987
Its width is f of x, its height is g of x
— so its area is exactly f times g.

19
00:01:05,087 --> 00:01:07,394
Now nudge x by a tiny amount, delta x.

20
00:01:07,424 --> 00:01:12,527
Both functions change: the width grows by
delta f, and the height grows by delta g.

21
00:01:12,627 --> 00:01:14,658
How much new area did we gain?

22
00:01:14,688 --> 00:01:15,551
Three pieces.

23
00:01:15,581 --> 00:01:18,162
A strip on the right: g times delta f.

24
00:01:18,192 --> 00:01:20,360
A strip on top: f times delta g.

25
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:23,177
And a tiny corner: delta f times delta g.

26
00:01:23,207 --> 00:01:25,769
Now watch what happens as delta x shrinks.

27
00:01:25,799 --> 00:01:31,107
The strips get thin — but the corner is
tiny times tiny, so it disappears much
faster.

28
00:01:31,447 --> 00:01:33,748
Let's turn the picture into algebra.

29
00:01:33,778 --> 00:01:38,280
The change in area is g delta f, plus f
delta g, plus delta f delta g.

30
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:40,417
Now divide everything by delta x.

31
00:01:40,447 --> 00:01:51,847
As delta x goes to zero, delta f over
delta x becomes f prime, delta g over
delta x becomes g prime, and the last term
becomes zero times g prime — which is
zero.

32
00:01:52,147 --> 00:01:55,987
What's left is the product rule: f prime
g, plus f g prime.

33
00:01:56,456 --> 00:02:02,026
Let's test it on a product we can check: h
of x equals x squared times x cubed.

34
00:02:02,056 --> 00:02:04,592
So f is x squared, with f prime two x.

35
00:02:04,622 --> 00:02:07,796
And g is x cubed, with g prime three x
squared.

36
00:02:08,096 --> 00:02:12,877
Plug into the rule: two x times x cubed,
plus x squared times three x squared.

37
00:02:12,907 --> 00:02:17,656
That's two x to the fourth plus three x to
the fourth — five x to the fourth.

38
00:02:17,956 --> 00:02:23,794
And the check: x squared times x cubed is
x to the fifth, whose derivative is five x
to the fourth.

39
00:02:23,824 --> 00:02:24,624
It matches!

40
00:02:24,916 --> 00:02:31,356
At x equals one, the two parts are two
times one, plus one times three: two plus
three, five.

41
00:02:31,709 --> 00:02:34,796
But what do that two and that three
actually mean?

42
00:02:34,826 --> 00:02:36,355
Go back to the rectangle.

43
00:02:36,385 --> 00:02:38,629
At x equals one, it's a unit square.

44
00:02:39,049 --> 00:02:44,899
Let's grow x by smaller and smaller steps,
and measure each new piece of area,
divided by delta x.

45
00:02:44,929 --> 00:02:49,785
The right strip heads to two, the top
strip heads to three, and the corner fades
to zero.

46
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:55,249
So the two is how fast area grows from the
width stretching, and the three, from the
height rising.

47
00:02:55,629 --> 00:03:00,049
That's the product rule: each factor's
rate of change, times the other factor.

48
00:03:00,329 --> 00:03:08,069
And it works even when you can't multiply
things out — like x squared sine x: two x
sine x, plus x squared cos x.

49
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:09,382
Three takeaways.

50
00:03:09,412 --> 00:03:14,073
One: the derivative of a product is not
the product of the derivatives.

51
00:03:14,103 --> 00:03:19,954
Two: the product rule, f prime g plus f g
prime, comes straight from a growing
rectangle.

52
00:03:19,984 --> 00:03:24,844
Three: the corner, delta f delta g,
vanishes because it's tiny times tiny.

53
00:03:24,874 --> 00:03:26,195
Thanks for watching!
